<p>Spain and Brazil used their first bilateral summit in Barcelona on April 17 to deepen ties and send a joint political message. Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez and Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva signed 15 agreements covering strategic sectors including critical minerals, telecommunications, and artificial intelligence. The two leaders also stood together against democratic backsliding, the far right, and tariff pressure linked to US President Donald Trump. That gave the summit a dual purpose: strengthening cooperation while signaling closer coordination between Europe and Latin America.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Anti-Trump framing: SCMP described the summit as part of Sánchez’s broader push to build partnerships beyond Europe in response to Donald Trump.</li><li>Foreign-policy tone: Lula used the meeting to praise Sánchez’s anti-war stance, showing the encounter was about more than trade and technology.</li><li>State-occasion staging: Mercopress said the Pedralbes Palace summit included military honors, underscoring the meeting’s ceremonial weight.</li><li>Beyond bilateral ties: Sánchez said the Spain-Brazil relationship goes “far beyond the strictly bilateral,” giving the summit a broader diplomatic purpose.</li></ul><blockquote>I understand when you say no to war - President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pedro Sánchez: The Spanish prime minister said peace and the values that sustain it are under attack, using the summit to link bilateral ties to a broader defense of the international order. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3350545/spain-signs-deals-brazil-sanchez-builds-anti-trump-bloc'>SCMP</a>)</li><li>Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva: The Brazilian president used the visit to warn that when democratic institutions weaken, extremist politics can return, giving the Barcelona trip a broader ideological message. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/hitler-appears-if-democracy-declines-brazils-president-lula'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Mercopress analysis: Mercopress portrayed the meeting as a cross-Atlantic alignment between center-left governments, stressing the symbolic value of a joint stand against the far right alongside the cooperation accords themselves. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/17/lula-and-sanchez-seal-progressive-alliance-in-barcelona-and-call-for-front-against-global-far-right'>MercoPress</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/hitler-appears-if-democracy-declines-brazils-president-lula'>‘Hitler’ appears if democracy declines: Brazil’s President Lula</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3350545/spain-signs-deals-brazil-sanchez-builds-anti-trump-bloc'>Spain signs deals with Brazil as Sanchez builds anti-Trump bloc</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/17/lula-and-sanchez-seal-progressive-alliance-in-barcelona-and-call-for-front-against-global-far-right'>Lula and Sanchez seal progressive alliance in Barcelona and call for front against global far right</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/17/i-understand-when-you-say-no-to-war-brazils-lula-tells-spains-pm-pedro-sanchez'>&#x27;I understand when you say no to war,&#x27; Brazil&#x27;s Lula tells Spain&#x27;s PM Pedro Sánchez</a> - euronews.com</li></ul>